#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198012 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198370 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "f794d50a-cb44-5a6f-a922-8438a6192d46" :question/math-academy-id "e-11157" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y)\\to(u(x,y), v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n![](images/b5/b52614b0fbaccee13747bedb98617811af7a4b13e64e14f761a16e6b274513ed.svg)\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/5e/5e8faca7bd98b1af626603c26aef5fc4d7a65064f4cdc3486670195fc29dff65.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ and $v(x,y) = e^{x-y}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & ={e}^{x-y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x+y}\\cdot (-{e}^{x-y})-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y}-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198042 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "217fe5bd-4ea4-57b9-8772-bb6e517207bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12416" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e97c59a7c736ec70142bc402566385fc2cd9c6aa78e304d70cefab1027078c1.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=0$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=-3.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem?\n\nI. $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n\nII. $f^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=0$\n\nIII. The derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y=-1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f: U \\to \\Bbb R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ $f(a) = b$ and $f'(a)\\neq 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *$f^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of $f^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(b) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(a)} =\\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x = -3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y = 0 = f(0).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 0$ since $f'(0) = 0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y = -1.1 = f(2),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 2.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=2,$ for example, $(0,4),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x = 2,$ i.e., $f'(2) \\neq 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y = -1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198234 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "d154ea1a-bb3c-510d-abe3-dc1cf51558b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7127" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as \n\n![](images/8d/8d64929af5483fd2a6517edbd292ffc01db4959430c13ddda6829e1da545c90e.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/a4/a4848c66ca7c3fa7a1f14e9d4b241a136e283f10ece91f19e689a22f03c92c3b.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $\\mathbf T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}  = 0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = u^2-v^2$ and $y(u,v) = u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -2v \\\\ \\,\\,1\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(u+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} =0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2(u+v) & =0 \\\\ u & =-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $T$ consist of those points that lie on the line $u=-v$  in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfe7ddf8-1d90-5732-b4e0-9b6cb53cfed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=-2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401181} {:answer/id #uuid "6b355cde-9299-54ff-9082-f28b3a088a6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=-2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401182} {:answer/id #uuid "a417c7cf-6062-5b00-9bc3-e0de419c5534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401183} {:answer/id #uuid "ac39e8c8-f1ca-5eec-9d60-f35deacbe886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=-v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401184} {:answer/id #uuid "0f39009f-2aaf-5fb6-b951-14bb531af992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f39009f-2aaf-5fb6-b951-14bb531af992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401185} :answer-field/id #uuid "003d00c4-a45e-580b-a2b2-1a8ab41f3386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6940edb0-4ff9-5079-afa0-d099bcae85dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-160465" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2v-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{e}^{u+v}(v-u) & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0 \\\\ u & =v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=v$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "879a74f3-cdb2-5fc9-83e0-82f1953090a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=6u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401188} {:answer/id #uuid "b598f6a3-a6bb-5202-8ed8-ac9c42eb7279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola $2{v}^{2}-3{u}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401189} {:answer/id #uuid "bca486aa-25ef-5c7c-9609-2ed1a4c5553a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=6.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401190} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3464e3-f53d-53d8-877c-0141084f3eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabola $2v=3{u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401191} {:answer/id #uuid "14c27fbe-cfe3-5924-a55c-3737ccd260a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{3}{2}u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14c27fbe-cfe3-5924-a55c-3737ccd260a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{3}{2}u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401192} :answer-field/id #uuid "753118cd-0a71-5bb3-aaee-283497ceb3e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f05de000-4a38-5b78-b8b6-ab15cb5a35af" :question/math-academy-id "q-160473" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2} \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}6u & 4v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6u\\cdot 1-4v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6u-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 6u-4v & =0 \\\\ v & =\\frac{3}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=\\frac{3}{2}u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198370 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f34e11-6208-5bd6-83d3-fb8f70484d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275683} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed05521-a389-5e3e-8ad1-0d982b3149dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276180} {:answer/id #uuid "5eff542f-2be2-5e3f-97b9-5d179c03100d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276327} {:answer/id #uuid "85bc8a32-6d26-5cda-87d2-f7f9c4c17889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276676} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4db293-b0be-5e3d-a7a4-81722a810570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ed05521-a389-5e3e-8ad1-0d982b3149dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276180} :answer-field/id #uuid "46fd785d-27e6-529a-8718-6d1dcb72fc65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6f65d80-7792-570e-9406-106101c7a70c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160563" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-6y \\\\ x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x+-y$ and $v(x,y)=x+3y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-6 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(-6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198370 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4969b933-9252-5d6c-99a3-2d0800a47ff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276127} {:answer/id #uuid "770127d5-5c20-5966-b228-70cc5f941617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276551} {:answer/id #uuid "b84e8d31-b0f2-5bc7-b250-aae11f693050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277111} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cce8a1-986b-5e0d-9d7a-5c50318cedd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277250} {:answer/id #uuid "d9290ebb-9e9d-5d8a-8ec2-a3ee34d8fd40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{v}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b84e8d31-b0f2-5bc7-b250-aae11f693050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277111} :answer-field/id #uuid "94528d39-40c7-5d22-bbd3-10b2d8db0c92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1077730f-94dd-5875-b9c2-642bfe60bb08" :question/math-academy-id "q-160582" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)={e}^{xy}$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y{e}^{xy} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{xy} & x{e}^{xy} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-x{e}^{xy}\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y{e}^{xy}}{x} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198370 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "632ae4e3-ee1f-5e8a-90cc-3116a07be361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2u}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479505} {:answer/id #uuid "6c54a42e-7de5-5fb7-b8c8-7174b039dd83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{2v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479523} {:answer/id #uuid "83b435cc-8b1e-5f1c-8aa8-a11fa47c493e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479552} {:answer/id #uuid "b019fb8d-4639-5879-8f60-c5aaf51eda53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{v}{2u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479622} {:answer/id #uuid "c815be35-c8ac-52ba-8f94-4f8209e87970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2v}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83b435cc-8b1e-5f1c-8aa8-a11fa47c493e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479552} :answer-field/id #uuid "4670dc2b-4eab-5b4e-ad02-4eea217ed995" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "52539dbe-37de-5500-97cf-839689e21baa" :question/math-academy-id "q-160585" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ x+y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and $v(x,y)=x+y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & -2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\cdot 1-(-2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (x+y)\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddcb03ab-ffc7-5dde-a933-7374df237b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449982} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3ecb14-3118-537c-b265-36ac3c5c4e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca3ecb14-3118-537c-b265-36ac3c5c4e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449983} :answer-field/id #uuid "2db9f7f0-2fbe-5bfb-88f5-650ddb13358c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450945} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bbec439-5029-5663-98a6-7e32d79eaa0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449984} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e4b64f-bd71-5313-87a7-f6c616739ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9e4b64f-bd71-5313-87a7-f6c616739ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449985} :answer-field/id #uuid "300d5e5c-ad59-5480-a520-0c1afdf364a1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450946} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec3ff93-228e-542f-8812-a0b344b17573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449986} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9c77c6-5431-5dbf-9223-a1c8fe1ec4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc9c77c6-5431-5dbf-9223-a1c8fe1ec4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449987} :answer-field/id #uuid "74c4ed37-cec0-5927-8a59-1ff0216677f6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b9d32a6-dbbc-54ba-9b9d-46f810008dd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161845" :question/problem "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The function $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n- **II.** The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n- **III.** The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement III is also true. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dfbfd1f-8c57-5fbe-a046-ceb729aa8a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471131} {:answer/id #uuid "73106404-c5ab-5410-828f-e3512801d032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dfbfd1f-8c57-5fbe-a046-ceb729aa8a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471131} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff7b3c6d-8c28-54e5-9c3d-44c52e635b0b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472071} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8d21e81-e466-5275-b6d7-e8596ffd6af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471133} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea8393e-8b7e-5757-8a39-9abbd88090d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8d21e81-e466-5275-b6d7-e8596ffd6af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471133} :answer-field/id #uuid "8576233d-a2de-53f9-8fb3-a010c3910212" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472072} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c413dcb0-496c-5788-83c8-a5743c676e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471135} {:answer/id #uuid "490e1529-0fed-5e20-985c-3761de5fd52f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "490e1529-0fed-5e20-985c-3761de5fd52f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471136} :answer-field/id #uuid "885433fd-1178-5da3-bd21-57f732fa2bb4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4dcab08e-c33d-5c73-a45a-265b0e425858" :question/math-academy-id "q-161847" :question/problem "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n- **I.** The function $f$ is invertible on $U.$\n- **II.** The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable on $U.$\n- **III.** The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$ which could be smaller than $U.$ \n\nFor example, the function $f(x)=2{x}^{4}$ satisfies the conditions of the inverse function theorem for $U=(-2,2)$ and $a=-1.$ However, $f(x)$ is not invertible on the entire set $U,$ only on a subset of $U.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the theorem, the function${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ which could be smaller than $U.$\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "385ce135-c231-5207-a3a6-8a8c0ed62b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479444} {:answer/id #uuid "6bdf97b1-4551-5dd2-8760-7f306190cd16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479520} {:answer/id #uuid "714e9dcf-1d76-5e20-ae52-149021021109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479528} {:answer/id #uuid "c19198c7-b862-5756-86f2-b383d975a476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479649} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ffeabc-77ac-5a3c-981c-16b319f409b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c19198c7-b862-5756-86f2-b383d975a476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479649} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0d9b917-d0b0-5b1f-9a90-f69dcef445f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bbe6736-f555-565a-b497-ef9dcdc55838" :question/math-academy-id "q-161848" :question/problem "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nI. The function $f$ is invertible in a neighborhood of $a.$\n\nII. The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\nIII. The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)={f}^{′}(a).$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15b10d6e-5a1e-5806-882e-1a82370b98d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479392} {:answer/id #uuid "1eba05a4-5234-5408-ae17-a7c92d57b021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479410} {:answer/id #uuid "2f50ad2a-df10-5aee-9bc1-a61707dbdb2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479431} {:answer/id #uuid "301b96b1-099b-590b-be3b-5f9c7fb06ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479434} {:answer/id #uuid "adf0e36f-9070-5c3f-b130-9a8e721211ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f50ad2a-df10-5aee-9bc1-a61707dbdb2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479431} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f711b0e-b282-5e5c-bddd-9a2334e0d40d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58964444-2a46-54cf-b2ee-de1f5f5033d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161864" :question/problem "![](images/53/539115fd470666cb84e3de4b9b3471db6f04d0af6fd3e33897e5115361fe4e7a.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=-3$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=1.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\nI. $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n\nII. ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-0.87$\n\nIII. The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=3$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-3$ since ${f}^{′}(-3)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $y=-0.87=f(-1),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=-1,$ for example, $(-2,0),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=-1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(-1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-0.87.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $y=3=f(1),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2c270a8-c691-513a-a6e6-0d9bc6a07b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465471} {:answer/id #uuid "f04baf77-b2d3-5f6d-b781-cd14f0b917ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2c270a8-c691-513a-a6e6-0d9bc6a07b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465471} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c053f18-2f32-5324-ad2d-52fdb23c6eb9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466579} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d895009-2af4-545d-b236-3a44449737f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465473} {:answer/id #uuid "e20845e9-b9fd-53c9-af91-08586cf71cc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d895009-2af4-545d-b236-3a44449737f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465473} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec10e473-f4ce-577c-b307-7b77d5e056be" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466580} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e1f5327-f511-5ec4-a32a-b794963bf007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465475} {:answer/id #uuid "0c52a783-530c-5154-a072-ed464f679923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c52a783-530c-5154-a072-ed464f679923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465476} :answer-field/id #uuid "a17b70a5-9aa2-55e8-b7fb-0e4865faf9bb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8eff559e-2582-52e2-ae0e-c082d3e9afb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161866" :question/problem "![](images/67/675226780bb9627524f4ab03691e84e312300f398f352abc3b698c1fcdd83490.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=-1$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=0$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=1$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=0$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y=1=f(-1).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-1$ since ${f}^{′}(-1)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y=0=f(2),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=2.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=2,$ for example, $(1,3),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=2,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(2)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=0$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d467be4-da9c-5949-964e-fc1ece22124b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u+v=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402618} {:answer/id #uuid "99d8765a-e6b9-5b6f-b95c-7ee236d3b208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u-v=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402619} {:answer/id #uuid "a23b6f7f-749d-5d1a-b619-0c32cdbf023d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0$ and $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402620} {:answer/id #uuid "128c4bf5-7f24-5412-a170-6a74863be1bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=-v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402621} {:answer/id #uuid "72a4bc7a-67e1-58b0-9c23-038668c40386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a23b6f7f-749d-5d1a-b619-0c32cdbf023d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0$ and $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402620} :answer-field/id #uuid "f05d1ca1-306f-5d0c-970a-e108a73f246e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48feab89-768b-5b8e-8c0a-02f86fdff070" :question/math-academy-id "q-38245" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-2v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-4uv-4uv \\\\  & =-8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ -8uv & =0 \\\\ uv & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $uv=0$ defines the pair of intersecting lines $u=0$ and $v=0$ in the $uv$-plane. Notice that these lines correspond to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b7083a-7ed8-5b67-9f27-7379156d8628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348647} {:answer/id #uuid "7140e241-25dc-5071-9fd8-4e1586f6ca35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349490} {:answer/id #uuid "8edc9fca-f595-593b-af4f-a043918e17a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349734} {:answer/id #uuid "93089cde-7bb5-5f53-8073-e850fa02dd2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349759} {:answer/id #uuid "a196b45d-6a7d-543c-ae9f-1e7c18a5a6ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17b7083a-7ed8-5b67-9f27-7379156d8628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348647} :answer-field/id #uuid "54321fa7-3493-59bf-9ddb-14a8621e896c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a6af403-e6c8-5c17-8e90-e6cdab8d3462" :question/math-academy-id "q-81169" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}uv \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=uv$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0 \\\\ v & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b035eef-7bbb-5b59-a594-3052f78e52d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348542} {:answer/id #uuid "2611c7d1-6820-5de7-8ccf-d3f5e4086430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348774} {:answer/id #uuid "3f97e89c-13cc-5818-90ba-411f82314bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=-2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349027} {:answer/id #uuid "65f9f69a-ed56-5750-b5d5-352d7eaf6058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola $uv=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349380} {:answer/id #uuid "a214a9ab-879a-5275-8800-eb572d8560bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=-2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b035eef-7bbb-5b59-a594-3052f78e52d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348542} :answer-field/id #uuid "31b3b169-dd0a-56f8-ab00-c926e96cc003" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ad5bfe8-2a30-5e85-98a8-d40233f4e08d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81274" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv} \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{uv}$ and $y(u,v)=u+2v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v{e}^{uv} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u{e}^{uv} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v{e}^{uv} & u{e}^{uv} \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v{e}^{uv}\\cdot 2-u{e}^{uv}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={e}^{uv}(2v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{uv}(2v-u) & =0 \\\\ 2v-u & =0 \\\\ u & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe equation $u=2v$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198370 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "272de190-0e96-5d51-95d4-ff366ae72e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348785} {:answer/id #uuid "62b96416-b661-53cd-8fd9-4a67cc99ae73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349349} {:answer/id #uuid "93ea44ae-a889-5898-8990-fdfc9599ae5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349770} {:answer/id #uuid "cefdca57-4e7f-505d-ba98-23b6018b411a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350265} {:answer/id #uuid "f22a24d4-0975-5c9c-9994-b66abef9c47f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62b96416-b661-53cd-8fd9-4a67cc99ae73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349349} :answer-field/id #uuid "6831b19c-e658-54e3-b4bb-f0560cf60cf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "24c29280-75c6-50b5-ad20-b15d70578fce" :question/math-academy-id "q-81305" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2xy \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2y\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-2x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4v}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198370 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f" :knowledge-point/title "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e0705a2-1588-5bf8-8ba6-300a7469e5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348710} {:answer/id #uuid "42ead89d-844f-5bca-a8ca-c47e27929a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349053} {:answer/id #uuid "4b357a7b-828a-5047-9f1e-d17b0308758b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349134} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2d84c2-3225-561a-8cbc-4bedf2fdc3ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349571} {:answer/id #uuid "d7464ed5-d467-5d05-a547-913262e8aeb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7464ed5-d467-5d05-a547-913262e8aeb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350334} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9031683-173b-5e87-9d80-7ac241be89df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a4f83b6-b23c-52c1-90bf-8de4914e1c48" :question/math-academy-id "q-81370" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x-4y \\\\ 2x+3y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=5x-4y$ and $v(x,y)=2x+3y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =5 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{23}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
